---
id: GS-002
type: rule
family: guardrail-suppression
title: Chained type assertion launders a value through `unknown`
version: 1

severity: warning
confidence: high
spectrum_position: 1

targets: [source]
file_extensions: [.ts, .tsx]
allowlist:
  [
    '**/*.test.ts',
    '**/*.test.tsx',
    '**/*.spec.ts',
    '**/*.spec.tsx',
    '**/__tests__/**',
    '**/__mocks__/**',
    '**/explain/**',
  ]

detection:
  type: regex
  pattern: '\bas\s+unknown\s+as\b'

related: [AP-004, GS-001, TE-001]
enabled: true
opt_in: false
---

`x as unknown as T` is two assertions doing one job: the first throws away
what the compiler knows about `x`, the second claims `T` in its place. The
detour through `unknown` exists only because `x as T` on its own was
rejected — TypeScript refused the conversion, so the code widened until the
refusal had nothing left to stand on.

Fix the mismatch instead of routing around it. If the value genuinely is a
`T`, narrow to it with a type guard or a schema parse and the single
assertion becomes unnecessary. If it is not a `T`, the chain is hiding a
real bug that will surface as a runtime `undefined` on the first property
access. If the two types are structurally unrelated on purpose — a branded
id, an opaque handle, a `readonly` array being handed to a mutating API —
that is a conversion worth naming: give it a small named function whose
body carries the one assertion and whose name says what is being converted.

## Why It's Harmful

TypeScript rejects `x as T` when `x` and `T` do not sufficiently overlap.
That rejection is the single most load-bearing check the assertion syntax
still performs — a plain `as` at least has to be *plausible*. Widening to
`unknown` first removes it, because every type is assignable to `unknown`
and `unknown` is assertable to everything. The result has the syntactic
weight of a cast and the safety of `@ts-ignore` (AP-004): whatever the
compiler was about to tell you, it now cannot.

It is also the shape that survives refactors silently. Rename a field on
`T`, delete it, change its type — a real conversion breaks and the compiler
points at it; a chained assertion keeps compiling and the failure moves to
runtime, usually far from the assertion that caused it. Every consumer
downstream inherits full confidence in a shape nobody checked.

Test doubles are the common legitimate use — building a partial mock and
presenting it as the full interface — so `*.test.ts`, `*.spec.ts`,
`__tests__/` and `__mocks__/` are allowlisted. In production code the
chain almost always marks the spot where a boundary should have been
parsed instead (TE-001).

Scope and known gaps: the detector matches the `as unknown as` token
sequence in the comment- and string-masked view, so the phrase in prose or
a string literal does not fire. The equivalent angle-bracket form
(`<T>(<unknown>x)`) is not matched — it is legacy syntax that collides with
JSX and does not appear in modern TypeScript. Laundering through a named
alias of `unknown` (`type Opaque = unknown; x as Opaque as T`) also evades
the token match. For a deliberate conversion that must stay, suppress with
the reason:
`// @anvil-ignore GS-002 -- branded id conversion, checked at the parse boundary`.
